WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.362
WHERE is the most familiar location for subqueries.

00:00:03.362 --> 00:00:10.737
The inner result can become a benchmark, a membership set, an existence condition, or a set comparison.

00:00:10.737 --> 00:00:15.552
The final job is always Boolean: should the current outer row remain?

00:00:15.552 --> 00:00:19.592
That is why many subquery patterns appear naturally in WHERE.

00:00:19.592 --> 00:00:24.422
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:24.422 --> 00:00:30.622
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:30.622 --> 00:00:33.047
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:33.047 --> 00:00:39.182
In a real setting such as an e-commerce team finding customers who purchased a target product,

00:00:39.182 --> 00:00:45.107
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:45.107 --> 00:00:47.417
before discussing syntax.

00:00:47.417 --> 00:00:53.680
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
